&lt;div class="sect1">
&lt;h2 id="_from_stateless_to_stateful_filters_composition">From Stateless to Stateful: Filters &amp;amp; Composition&lt;/h2>
&lt;div class="sectionbody">
&lt;div class="paragraph">
&lt;p>In previous parts, we built an oscillator. It was &amp;#34;stateless&amp;#34;—you give it a phase, it gives you a value. It doesn’t care
what happened a microsecond ago. But to make music sound &amp;#34;warm&amp;#34; or &amp;#34;organic,&amp;#34; we need objects that have a &lt;strong>memory&lt;/strong>.&lt;/p>
&lt;/div>
&lt;div class="sect2">
&lt;h3 id="_1_dsp_general_the_concept_of_state">1. DSP General: The Concept of State&lt;/h3>
&lt;div class="paragraph">
&lt;p>In Digital Signal Processing (DSP), a filter is &lt;strong>stateful&lt;/strong>. To calculate the &lt;strong>current&lt;/strong> output, a filter needs to know
the &lt;strong>previous&lt;/strong> output.&lt;/p>
&lt;/div></description></item></channel></rss>